oaxaca-desktop (4.0.4+git20261022.120.51e652c5-0oaxaca1) stonking; urgency=medium

  * Monitor scale is set per monitor: the Display panel's scale buttons and
    Super + / save the scale as a rule for the focused monitor in
    ~/.config/hypr/monitors.lua. They wrote it into the catch-all rule, and
    Hyprland's reload of the changed file rescaled every other monitor too.
  * The Display panel's text size header reads "TEXT SIZE · ALL DISPLAYS"
    with more than one monitor on: text size is one setting for the shell,
    GTK and the terminals, while scale is per monitor.
  * Setup > Defaults > Terminal lists Wave once Wave is installed, and
    choosing Wave explains in a notification why Wave cannot be the default
    terminal: Wave does not run a command given at launch, which the menu's
    terminal tools rely on. Installing Wave from Install > Terminal sends
    the same notification.
  * Install > Development > GitHub CLI installs gh from GitHub's apt
    repository, as GitHub's instructions for Debian and Ubuntu describe,
    replacing a github-cli.list made by hand from those instructions;
    Remove > Development > GitHub CLI removes gh and the repository.
  * A new user's setup finishes on a first login without network: the
    Node.js download no longer stops it, so the tool stubs (gh, codex and
    the rest), the default browser and the setup's completion marker
    follow. A setup that still fails is written to first-run.log and
    retried at the next login instead of passing unnoticed.
  * Install > Development > AWS CLI installs the aws-cli snap with classic
    confinement; Remove > Development > AWS CLI removes it and keeps ~/.aws.
  * Setup > Monitors opens nwg-displays, saving its layout to
    ~/.config/hypr/monitors-gui.lua, and so does nwg-displays' entry in the
    app launcher, which would otherwise have written over monitors.lua;
    Setup > Config > Monitors opens monitors.lua in the editor. The scale
    buttons and Super + / also change the scale in nwg-displays' rule,
    which Hyprland applies last.
  * fzf's Ctrl+R history search and ** completion load in the shell: they
    came from Arch's /usr/share/fzf paths, which Ubuntu's fzf does not use.
  * Remove > Security > Fingerprint and Fido2 show once set up: they looked
    for the PAM modules in /etc/pam.d/sudo, where Ubuntu has only
    @include common-auth.

